    "lawCode": "Amd §§403 & 408, add §408-b, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6307               REVISED 06/10/19\n\nSPONSOR: BENJAMIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the general business law, in relation\nto requiring domestic violence and sexual assault awareness education\nfor persons engaged in the practice of nail specialty, waxing, natural\nhair styling, esthetics and cosmetology\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill requires the Department of State to make available to appear-\nance enhancement professionals a one-hour training course in domestic\nviolence and sexual assault education as part of their license renewal\nprocess.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends subdivision 1 of section 406 of the general business\nlaw by adding a new paragraph.\n\nSection two amends subdivision 6 of section 408 of the general business\nlaw, as amended by section 1 of part Y of chapter 60 of the laws of\n2011.\n\nSection three amends the general business law by adding a new section\n408-b. Section four sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn the United States, nearly 20 individuals experience physical domestic\nabuse per minute. Domestic abusers and those who commit sexual assault\nheavily monitor the whereabouts and daily interactions of their partners\nas a way to control their victim. However, most domestic abuse and sexu-\nal assault victims are not monitored during trips to beauty salons.\nThose in the beauty industry, such as hair stylists, nail specialists,\nand other cosmetologists, build close relationships with their clients\nthrough routine visits. These relationships may result in the disclosure\nof personal information from clients, as their stylist serves as a\nneutral party.\n\nThis bill would provide appearance enhancement professionals with the\nopportunity to complete a voluntary one-hour training course in domestic\nviolence and sexual assault, as part of their license renewal process.\nThis course would provide instruction on how to help victims in danger-\nous situations and provide them with the appropriate resources to seek\nhelp. This training would not require reports of incidents to law\nenforcement, but rather provide education and increased awareness to\nappearance enhancement professionals. The department shall be required\nto make this training available to all licensees and applicants for a\nlicense or renewal.\n\nAs the epidemic of domestic violence and sexual assault persists, it is\nimperative that New York find creative ways to effectively address the\nissue. To date, a similar bill has become law in Illinois.  This bill\nwill empower victims to seek help and escape the cycle of abuse without\nfear of detrimental repercussions.\n\n  This  bill  would  require  appearance  enhancement  professionals  to\ncomplete a one-hour training course  in  domestic  violence  and  sexual\nassault,  as  part  of  their license renewal process. This course would\nprovide instruction on how to help victims in dangerous  situations  and\nprovide  them with the appropriate resources to seek help. This training\nwould not require reports of incidents to law  enforcement,  but  rather\nprovide  education  and  increased  awareness  to appearance enhancement\nprofessionals.\n\nAs the epidemic of domestic violence and sexual assault persists, it  is\nimperative  that  New York find creative ways to effectively address the\nissue. To date, a similar bill has become law in Illinois.    This  bill\nwill  empower victims to seek help and escape the cycle of abuse without\nfear of detrimental repercussions.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: A.6576-B - Advanced to Third Reading; S.9028-A  -  Referred  to\nRules\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis  act  shall  take  effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become a law.\n",
